Clean-room statement
--------------------

This cartridge was authored from a recorded final-result baseline — measured
dimensions, mesh statistics and a written specification — without access to any
prior implementation of this slug or to its upstream. The implementer did not
read, and did not have available:

  * the previous `multiboard` cartridge in this repository's git history;
  * the archived `madfam-org/multiboard` satellite repository, or any checkout
    of it;
  * the Multiboard project (Keep Making) — its website, its model files, its
    CAD sources, or any remix of them on a model-sharing site.

Every line of `tile.scad` and every value in `project.json` was written from the
baseline pack and from the OpenSCAD language reference.

**This cartridge is NOT derived from the Multiboard design files.**

What is the interface, and why it matches
-----------------------------------------

Interoperability is the point of a mounting grid: an accessory printed by
someone else must screw into this panel. The *functional* dimensions other
objects mate to are therefore implemented to the measured values, and are frozen
constants in `tile.scad` rather than user parameters:

  * 25.0 mm grid pitch, both axes, both hole classes;
  * cell-centre bore — internal trapezoidal thread, 29° flank, 2.5 mm pitch,
    major Ø 22.54 mm, minor Ø 20.15 mm;
  * interior-node bore — internal trapezoidal thread, 29° flank, 3 mm pitch,
    major Ø 6.95 mm, minor Ø 4.48 mm;
  * panel thickness (the `height` parameter, 6.4 mm by default) is the thread
    engagement length an accessory screws into.

These are dimensions of a mechanical fit, taken by measurement. They are stated
here so anyone can verify them against a physical panel; no upstream file was
consulted to obtain them.

The thread is a true helix
--------------------------

Both thread classes are real helices cut at full panel depth, not stacks of
concentric rings. A ring stack can be dimensionally exact and still be useless:
a screw cannot thread into it. Each bore is one `linear_extrude` of the
trapezoidal tooth profile with `twist = -360 · height / pitch`, which is a helix
of exactly that pitch by construction.

`docs/CLEANROOM-VERIFICATION.md` records the proof: 8-angle radial sampling per
z-slice shows the minor-diameter band advancing 144.4°/mm on the primary bore
(lead 2.494 mm/turn against a declared 2.5 mm pitch) and 121.0°/mm on the
secondary (2.975 mm/turn against 3 mm). A revolved ring stack would read 0°/mm,
and each bore turns at its OWN declared rate, which a sampling artefact could
not produce.

An earlier attempt implemented this mode in CadQuery. It was abandoned on render
cost: the B-Rep boolean of the helical thread tools against the plate took 376
seconds at the default 4 × 4 tile and left the plate fragmented rather than one
solid. OpenSCAD's Manifold backend cuts the same true helices from the same
plate in tens of seconds and yields one watertight body, so this mode is
OpenSCAD — which is also the engine the manifest contract declared.

What is the form, and why it differs
------------------------------------

The upstream tile design is licensed CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0, a licence that covers the
*design itself* and not merely code. Under ADR-021 §4 the acceptance target for
this slug is therefore the interface standard with a form of our own — not a
shape match. The following are MADFAM's design and differ deliberately:

  * **Silhouette.** The tile perimeter is castellated: a rounded tab stands
    proud of the rectangle at every grid node on the boundary, so a seam
    connector at an edge node sits in solid material with metal all round it.
    The baseline is a plain rectangle.
  * **Corner treatment.** The four plate corners take a 45° flat of a fixed
    6 mm, decoupled from the grid module. The baseline's corner treatment was an
    octagonal chamfer scaled *from* the cell size; ours is independent of it by
    design.
  * **Rear relief.** Every cell-centre bore is coned on the back face so a screw
    starts square without a bench chamfer. The baseline has no such relief.

`docs/CLEANROOM-VERIFICATION.md` records the measured interface dimensions of
every rendered variant against the baseline, and the form dimensions that
differ.

Two corrections to the baseline record
--------------------------------------

The baseline pack's prose disagrees with its own measurements in two places, and
this cartridge follows the measurements — they are the record of what was
actually rendered and measured.

  * **Secondary bore positions.** The pack's prose says one bore per cell corner,
    (x+1)(y+1) = 25 at the 4 x 4 default; its measured record says 9, which is
    (x-1)(y-1) — the INTERIOR intersections only. Nine is also the only reading
    the geometry allows: a bore centred on a boundary node would be half off the
    plate. This cartridge places secondary bores at interior intersections.
  * **Thread nominals.** The prose gives the primary major as 22.5 mm and the
    secondary major as 7.025 mm; the measured record gives 22.537 and 6.948, and
    the prose's own interface table agrees with the measurements rather than
    with its tolerance section. This cartridge targets the measured values.
    Building to the 7.025 figure would have put the secondary major 0.077 mm off
    the measured standard — outside the 0.05 mm interface tolerance.

Defect fixed rather than reproduced
-----------------------------------

The baseline exposed a `cell_size` parameter over 20–35 mm while the thread
diameters stayed fixed. Only 25 mm produced valid geometry: at 20 mm the Ø22.54
bore exceeded the cell and fragmented the plate into 25 separate bodies; at
35 mm the result was non-manifold with 52 bodies. This cartridge keeps the
parameter — 25 mm IS the interface, and above it the same bores simply sit
further apart, which is valid geometry — but clamps it at the geometric floor of
25 mm rather than fragmenting, and `project.json` carries a `severity: error`
constraint that says so in the UI before a render is queued. Every point in the
declared range now produces one watertight body, which is the commons standard
and which the baseline did not meet.
